\section{Discussion and Conclusions}
In this work we explore humor in the context of the popular card game Cards Against Humanity.
We introduce a novel dataset of 300,000 online games and 785K unique jokes, analyze it and provide insights. We trained state-of-the-art machine learning models to predict the winning joke per game. Interestingly, we find that past performance of the punchline card is a very strong indicator (unrelated to the prompt), and that short and crude punchlines tend to win. We show our models primarily focus on punchline, and observe potential  {behavioral biases} in the data. %, with the prompt card having little impact on them.  
%achieving performance twice as good (20\%) as random, even without any information about the users. %, and can somewhat generalize to novel jokes. 
On the more difficult task of judging novel cards, we see the models' ability to generalize is moderate, leaving room for future work. 
%Interestingly, we find that funniness is primarily determined by the punchline card alone, with the prompt card having little impact on players' preference. 
%Analyzing feature importance, we observe that short and crude punchlines tend to win.
We believe humor is a crucial component in developing
personable human-computer interactions, and the CAH dataset has several characteristics rendering
it particularly attractive for NLP research. We hope it will promote further work in this area.

%We have set out to automatically pick the funniest punchline in a Cards Against Humanity game. 
%Surprisingly, we found that the best models used only the punchline texts, with no significant feature contributions from the context (the prompt)ץ

%, as measured by feature importance and ablation testing (removing the prompt text as an input for the autoML feature extractor).

