Concerning the detection, we choosed to work with a CMOS Camera from HAMAMATSU (Orca-Flash 4.0 LT Plus) with a pitch of 6.5 $\mu$m and a maximum Quantum Efficiency of 82 $\%$ at 560 nm. All of that will enable us to discern the specific effects of each parameter (configuration type, scintillator type, scintillator thickness) on the performance of the TP, such as the signal to noise ratio on localisation detector, the width of the traces, their possible discrimination, and thus the associated energy resolution.

For each configuration involving a scintillator, two different thicknesses (0.1 and 1 mm) will be tested to assess the impact of this parameter on the results. Additionally, three different types of scintillators (organic, phosphor, and inorganic) will be simulated, with their corresponding data presented in Table~\ref{tab:1}. It is important to notice that EJ-444 consists of a thin piece of EJ-212 plastic scintillator (0.1 or 1 mm in our case) with a layer ($\approx$ 40 $\mu$m) of silver activated zinc sulfide phosphor (ZnS:Ag) applied to one side.

