\section{Introduction and Main Result}~\label{sec:intro}
Ever since \'{E}mile Borel introduced the concept of normality and showed that almost every number is normal \cite{Bor09}, generations of mathematicians have worked hard to unravel the secrets of normal numbers. Although many profound statements about the structure of normal numbers have been shown \cite{Cas59, Bug12, Lev79, Rau76, Sir17, Schm62} and in recent years there has been increasing interest in the computability of concrete normal numbers \cite{ABSS17,BF02,BHS13}, the most important conjectures concerning normal numbers remain unsolved. In particular, it currently seems unfeasible to show that important mathematical constants like $\sqrt{2}, e$ or $ \pi$ are normal. 

In contrast, the field of transcendental number theory has achieved significant breakthroughs: although many conjectures such as Schanuel's conjecture regarding transcendental numbers remain open, Hermite, Lindemann and Weierstrass proved in their seminal works the transcendence of $e$ and $\pi$ over a century ago \cite{Her73,Hil93,Lin82,Wei85}. This disparity raises a natural question: why is it considerably harder to prove a number is normal than to prove it is transcendental?  If one understands a proof of normality as a demonstration that a number is not non-normal, the answer seems to be that the set of non-normal numbers is  of Hausdorff dimension 1, while the non-transcendental numbers, i.e. the algebraic numbers, are only a countable set. This note presents another perspective on the complexity of non-normal numbers: \textit{There is no nowhere constant continuous function that maps all non-normal numbers to normal numbers}. This illustrates the intricate geometry of the set of non-normal numbers, potentially explaining the difficulty of proving normality.

To set the stage, we recall first some standard terminology. We say that a real number $x \in \rr$ is simply $b$-normal for some integer $b \geq 2$ if all digits $\{0,1,\ldots, b-1\}$ are asymptotically equidistributed in the $b$-ary representation of $x$. If not just the digits but all finite words $w \in \bigcup_{k = 1}^{\infty}\{0,1,\ldots, b-1\}^k$ appear with asymptotic frequency $b^{-|w|}$ in the $b$-ary decomposition, we call $x$ a $b$-normal number. Finally, $x$ is an (absolutely) normal number if $x$ is $b$-normal with respect to any integer basis $b \geq 2$. We denote the respective sets by $\mathcal{N}_{b,s}, \mathcal{N}_b$ and $\mathcal{N}$. For a set $A \subset \rr$ we set $A^{c} := \rr \setminus A$.

Our results also needs the concept of nowhere constant functions:

\begin{definition}
 Let $I \subset \rr$ be a non-empty open interval and $\varphi : I \to \rr$ some function. We say that $\varphi$ is non-constant at $t_0 \in I$ if for any $\varepsilon > 0 $
 \begin{equation}
    \varphi((t_0 - \varepsilon, t_0 + \varepsilon)) \setminus\{\varphi(t_0) \} \neq \emptyset.
 \end{equation}
 The function $\varphi$ is nowhere constant (in $I$) if it is  non-constant at any $t \in I.$
\end{definition}

Note that for a function $\varphi$ to be nowhere constant it is sufficient that the set
$$ D := \left\{ x \in I \, \bigg| \, \liminf_{h \to 0} \left|\frac{\phi(x+h) - \phi(x)}{h}\right| > 0 \right\} $$
is dense in $I$. In particular, all typical realizations of Brownian motions are nowhere constant continuous functions.

Our main result reads as follows:

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
    Let $I \subset \rr$ be some non-empty open interval and $\varphi \colon I \to \rr$ a nowhere constant continuous function. Then, for any base $ b \geq 2$
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
    \varphi(I \cap \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c}) \cap \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c} \neq \emptyset. 
    \end{equation}
\end{theorem}

Of course, \eqref{eq:main} directly yields 
$$ \varphi(I \cap \mathcal{N}^{c}) \cap \mathcal{N}^{c} \neq \emptyset, $$
which motivates the title of this note. Two remarks are in order.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:main} to $\varphi(x) = e^x$ and to all open intervals $I = (\alpha, \beta) \subset \rr$, one concludes that the set of numbers $x$ for which $x$ and $e^x$ are not normal is a dense subset of $\rr$. To our knowledge, so far there has been no algorithm which generates a non-normal number $x \neq 0$ for which $e^x$ is not normal. Our proof, however, can easily be turned to an algorithm for a given function. Note the remarkable difference compared to the case of algebraicity: due to the celebrated Lindemann-Weierstrass Theorem $e^x$ is transcendental for every algebraic $x \neq 0$.
    \item Let us discuss some possible extensions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)]  Theorem~\ref{thm:main} might even hold under the weaker condition that $\varphi$ is just a continuous non-constant function. However, our proof fails in this situation and the stronger  Theorem~\ref{thm:main2} might be become false for non-constant functions. 
        \item[(ii)] We conjecture that Theorem~\ref{thm:main} still holds true for any countable family $(\varphi_k)_{k\in \nn}$ of nowhere constant continuous function $\varphi_k :I \to \rr: $
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:mainfamily}
    \left(\bigcap_{k = 1}^{\infty} \varphi_k(I \cap \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c}) \right)\cap \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c} \neq \emptyset. 
    \end{equation}
    This would have the interesting consequence that the set of numbers $x$, for which all numbers $(e^{\alpha x})_{\alpha \neq 0 \text{ algebraic}}$ are non-normal, forms a dense set. 
    \item[(iii)] Moreover, even in the setting of a single nowhere constant function $\varphi$ we suspect that \eqref{eq:main} can be further sharpened:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:mainbasis}
     \varphi\left(I \cap \bigcap_{b = 2}^{\infty}  \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c}\right) \cap \left(\bigcap_{b = 2}^{\infty} \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c} \right) \neq \emptyset. 
    \end{equation}
    \end{itemize} 
    While a proof of (i) definitely needs new ideas, we believe that both \eqref{eq:mainfamily} and \eqref{eq:mainbasis} can be derived by a more elaborate version of our proof strategy. We hope to address these possible extensions in future work.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{comment}
    We argue that the assertion \eqref{eq:main} should not be seen as a reflection of the size of non-normal numbers but rather as facet of its complex structure.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)] In the proof below (see Proposition), we in fact construct a set $\mathcal{Z}_b \subset \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c}$ of Hausdorff dimension zero such that 
        $$ \varphi(I \cap \mathcal\mathcal{Z}_b) \cap \mathcal{Z}_b \neq \emptyset $$
        for any interval $I$ and function $\varphi$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
        \item[(ii)] Let us construct a (not so pathological) set of full measure for which Theorem~\ref{thm:main} fails. We set for any basis $b \geq 2$
        \begin{align*}
            A_b := \{ x \in \rr \, | \,  &\text{ all digits } d \in \{0,1,\ldots,b-1\} \text{ appear infinitely} \\ &\text{often in the } b\text{-ary representation of }x \}
        \end{align*}
        and $A_{2,3} := A_2 \cap A_3$ will serve as our example. Of course, $A_{2,3}$ is of full measure. Let $I = (0,1)$ and $C:(0,1) \to (0,1)$ be the Cantor function, which is well-known to be continuous and bijective. We claim that 
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:cantor}
            C(I \cap A_{2,3}) \cap A_{2,3} = \emptyset.
        \end{equation}
        Let $x = 0. x_1 x_2 x_3 \ldots$ be the trinary representation of $x \in (0,1) \cap A_{2,3}$. Since $x \in A_{2,3}$, there exists a minimal index $m \in \nn$ s.t. $x_m = 1$. By definition of the Cantor function the binary representation $C(x) =:y = 0. y_1 y_2 y_3 \cdots$ is given by
        $$ y_i := \begin{cases}
            0, & \text{ if }  i > m, \\
            \min\{y_i,1\}, & \text{ if } i \leq m. 
        \end{cases}$$
        We note that the binary representation of $y$ has only finitely many $1$s, i.e., $y \neq {A_2}$. Hence, \eqref{eq:cantor} follows.
    \end{itemize}
\end{comment}

In our second main result, we want to argue that Theorem~\ref{thm:main} should not be seen as a reflection of the sheer size of $\mathcal{N}^c$, but rather as a facet of the complicated geometry of $\mathcal{N}^c$. To this end, it is convenient to give the discussed property a name

\begin{definition}
    We call a set $A \subset \rr$ super-dense, if for any non-empty open interval $I$ and any nowhere constant continuous function $\varphi : I \to \rr$ one has
    \begin{equation}
        \varphi(I \cap A) \cap A \neq \emptyset. 
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}

This concept might already exist in the literature under a different name. Clearly, a super-dense set is dense in the usual sense with respect to the Euclidean metric; and it is not hard to see that a super-dense set is necessarily uncountable. 
Our second  result is

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main2}
   For any integer $b \geq 2$, there exists a super-dense set $\mathcal{Z}_b \subset \mathcal{N}_{b,s}^{c}$ of Hausdorff dimension zero. On the other hand, the set of absolutely normal numbers $\mathcal{N} $  is not super-dense. 
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} directly follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:main2}, and a proof of the latter is presented in the next Section~\ref{sec:proof}, where we also give an explicit definition of $\mathcal{Z}_b$ in \eqref{eq:zero}. It is very surprising that the non-normal numbers are in some sense "denser" or "richer" than the absolutely normal numbers $\mathcal{N}$, which form a set of full measure. Our interpretation is that the geometry of $\mathcal{N}$ is simpler as $\mathcal{N}$ only contains numbers which behave generically, whereas  $\mathcal{N}^{c}$ encodes many fractal-like subsets. Finally, we want to mention that although the notion of super-dense sets seems to be related to the topological concept of non-meager sets (also often referred to as of the second category), it is not clear to us if both terms are equivalent as meager sets are only stable under homeomorphisms. Similarly to our Theorem~\ref{thm:main2} it is known that very small subsets of $\mathcal{N}^c$ are non-meager whereas the set of normal numbers $\mathcal{N}$ is in fact meager \cite{Ol04a,Ol04b,AL23}. It would be desirable to better understand the relation between super-density and the Baire categories.


