\begin{abstract}
\noindent The hydrodynamic attractors paradigm aims to explain fast applicability of hydrodynamics at ultrarelativistic nuclear collisions at RHIC and LHC in terms of the emergence of a universal behaviour across different initial states. The attractors observed in theoretical models are first driven by a rapid expansion of matter, which later gives way to the decay of exponentially damped transient excitations into the viscous fluid regime. Recently a theoretical proposal was made how to realize hydrodynamic attractors in cold atomic gases focusing on the decay of transients and opening prospects for a future experimental realization in table top experiments. Motivated by this, in the present paper we show how the far-from-equilibrium,  expansion-driven part of the hydrodynamic attractor emerges in a model of a nearly-unitary Fermi gas dynamics under full theoretical control.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\noindent \textbf{\emph{Introduction.--}} Understanding thermalization of closed quantum systems is an important contemporary research direction across energy scales and across theoretical landscape~\cite{Berges:2020fwq,Ueda:2020ehs}. Our work is motivated by possibilities to realize nonequilibrium phenomena from the domain of high energies in cold atom experiments and earlier successes on this front for e.g. nonthermal fixed points~\cite{Prufer:2018hto,Erne:2018gmz} and elliptic flow~\cite{Brandstetter:2023jsy}.

The focal idea in our paper is that of hydrodynamic attractors~\cite{Soloviev:2021lhs,Jankowski:2023fdz}. This is a theoretical paradigm born out of efforts to understand the emergence of fluidity under extreme conditions, apparent in phenomenological modeling of ultrarelativistic nuclear collisions at RHIC and LHC accelerators. There, relativistic generalizations of Navier-Stokes equations model the nuclear medium in terms of its coarse properties such as energy density, fluid velocity and nonequilibrium contributions to pressure varying over the fireball~\cite{Busza:2018rrf,Schenke:2021mxx}. From the point of view of microscopic dynamics, the emergence of such regularities was not obvious and called for a better understanding.%Given that the microscopic dynamics governed by quantum field theory is vastly more complicated, the fact that

The appearance of hydrodynamic attractors in models of nuclear collisions can be viewed as a three stage process:
\begin{itemize}
\item \underline{STAGE E(xpansion):} The matter created in the very early stages of nuclear collisions exhibits initially strong expansion along the collision axis. In kinetic theory, as well as in equations of motion used to model relativistic fluid mechanics, this fast expansion has been observed to act as a very efficient memory loss mechanism about the details of the initial state~\cite{Blaizot:2017ucy}. This stage is the least understood of all three, for example, results of~\cite{Kurkela:2019set} indicate it is not realized when microscopic interactions are strong enough. As a consequence, it is the most interesting one to try to get a handle on experimentally.
\item \underline{STAGE T(ransients):} Near-equilibrium hydrodynamics is approached in an exponential fashion by a fast decay of transient excitations. There are both higher-level reasons for the presence of this stage~\cite{Heller:2021yjh}, as well as support for its emergence from ab initio simulations~\cite{Chesler:2009cy,Heller:2014wfa,Du:2022bel}.
\item \underline{STAGE H(ydrodynamics):} At late enough time, the near-equilibrium hydrodynamics emerges, which is determined by a handful of transport coefficients such as shear and bulk viscosity~\cite{Florkowski:2017olj}. This stage is by now rather well understood.
\end{itemize}
The understanding outlined above is largely based on a model of nuclear collision introduced by Bjorken~\cite{Bjorken:1982qr} in which the post-collision dynamics depends only on time and position along the collision axis via their boost invariant combination. Moreover, most of the studies were pursued in systems invariant under conformal symmetry where energy is the only scale in equilibrium. Despite its simplicity, the Bjorken flow model captures the crucial early time physics dominated by the expansion along the collision axis and offers systematic ways to improve upon by incorporating additional physical effects into hydrodynamic attactors, such as transverse expansion~\cite{Romatschke:2017acs,Ambrus:2021sjg,Ambrus:2021fej,Ambrus:2022koq,An:2023yfq}. Also, breaking the conformal symmetry is a feature that can be achieved in a controllable manner in a handful of setups~\cite{Romatschke:2017acs}. 

Upon adopting comoving coordinates, one can view the Bjorken flow as dynamics depending on a single coordinate, proper time $\tau$, in a simple spacetime geometry in which one direction expands in a power law manner with proper time. The interpretation led not only to studies of hydrodynamic attractors in other expanding geometries, see e.g.~\cite{Mitra:2024zfy}, but also led to a recent idea how to realize hydrodynamic attractor in cold atomic gases~\cite{Fujii:2024yce}.

Building on earlier results in~\cite{Fujii:2018aik}, the authors of~\cite{Fujii:2024yce} considered a Fermi gas close to unitarity with time-dependent scattering length $a(t)$. This excites hydrodynamic excitations in a similar way in which a uniformly expanding spacetime geometry does. The resulting equation of motion for nonequilibrium contribution to pressure $\Pi$ takes then the form
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:pievo}
    \tau_\Pi\dot\Pi=-\Pi-\zeta\, V[a(t)]
\end{equation}
where $\tau_{\Pi}$ is the relaxation time, $\zeta$ the bulk viscosity and 
\begin{equation}
V[a(t)]=3a(t)\partial_ta^{-1}(t)\,.
\end{equation}
The presence of the hydrodynamic tail governed by $\Pi\approx-\zeta V[a(t)]$ supplemented with the exponentially governed excitations obeying $\tau_\Pi\dot\Pi\approx-\Pi$ make this setup realize STAGE T and STAGE H of hydrodynamic attractor~\cite{Fujii:2024yce}. One interesting interplay with the experiment already at this point are encouraging results in~\cite{Brewer:2015ipa} on exponentially decaying transients in the data obtained in~\cite{Kinast:2005zz,Vogt:2011np}. 

The aim of our article is to demonstrate that the equations of motion describing the dynamics of a near-unitary Fermi gas, which include~\eqref{eq:pievo}, predict the emergence of the most interesting STAGE E of hydrodynamic attractors. As we will show, this happens for values of $\tau_{\Pi}$ and $\zeta$ obtained from controllable microscopic derivation, which paves way for a possible detection and further studies in experiment.

Throughout this paper, we set $\hbar=k_B=1$.

