\noindent \textbf{\emph{Hydrodynamic attractors as fixed points for cold atoms}} In the case of Bjorken flow, arguably the most attractive picture of hydrodynamic attractors is that of an interpolation between early and late time fixed points~\cite{Blaizot:2017ucy,Blaizot:2019scw}. Let us provide a perspective on how such fixed points emerge generally in evolution equations of one unknown in one variable. This perspective will be crucial for the understanding of STAGE E of hydrodynamic attractors in a cold Fermi gas close to unitarity.

Consider a quantity $f(t)$ that obeys a first order evolution equation
\begin{align}
    \partial_tf=G(f,t)\;.
\end{align}
After grouping the terms in $G(f,t)$ by their dependence on $t$, we consider each of these groups separately. If one of these time dependence terms dominates all others on a certain timescale, then close to that timescale $f$ evolves according to
\begin{align}
    \partial_t f =T(t) F(f)\;,
\end{align}
where $T(t)$ denotes the time dependence in question and $F(f)$ collects all dependences on $f$ in the terms with this time dependence. In this situation, we can solve for $f(t)$ by separation of variables. Let us also assume that $T(t)$ is positive. Then the general behaviour of $f(t)$ on this timescale can be predicted from simple properties of $F(f)$. 
\begin{itemize}
\item \underline{$F(f)=\mathrm{const}$:} The evolution of $f$ does not depend on its initial state. $f$ changes by a fixed amount given by the time integral of $\mathrm{const}\cdot T(t)$. In particular, there is no information loss mechanism.
\item \underline{$F(f_0)=0$, $F$ decreasing/increasing around $f_0$:} In the vicinity of $f_0$, deviations of $f$ from $f_0$ will decay/grow. This means that on the given timescale, $f_0$ is an attractive/repulsive fixed point.
\item \underline{$F(f_0)=0$, $F(f)>0$/$F(f)<0$ around $f_0$:} $f_0$ is a fixed point that is attractive from below/above and repulsive from above/below.
\end{itemize}

We can also conclude that the attractor basin of a given attractive fixed point extends to the next zero of~$F$. Furthermore, the line of arguments given above holds also in cases where the evolution of $f$ is coupled to that of another dynamical variable, so long as it is nonzero and has a time dependence of a fixed form if $f$ is close to $f_0$.

In the case of Bjorken flow, there are expansion-type terms with a time dependence $~1/\tau$ and relaxation-type terms with no explicit time dependence. The former dominate at early times, while the latter dominate at late times. Applying the above reasoning to both terms, one finds a repulsor and an attractor at early times, and an attractor to zero shear at late times~\cite{Heller:2015dha}.

Now let us consider the evolution of bulk pressure $\Pi$ according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:pievo}. It only has two terms, one that corresponds to the bulk stress due to changes in $a$ an one that facilitates the decay of $\Pi$ to the momentaneous bulk stress. With the given form of $a(t)$~\eqref{eq.formofexp}, the former term has a $t$-dependence $\propto t^{-2\alpha-1}$ and no $\Pi$-dependence, while the latter is constant in time and proportional to~$\Pi$. This means that for sufficiently large $a_0^{-1}$ the evolution of $\Pi$ can be divided into two phases. At early times, the stress coming from $a(t)$ builds up a certain amount of bulk pressure, independent of the initial state. At times $\mathcal{O}(\tau_\Pi)$, $\Pi$ decays to its Navier-Stokes value, i.e. $-\zeta\,V[a(t)]$. Only the latter constitutes universal attractor behaviour, corresponding to equilibration.


%\noindent \textbf{\emph{Modifying evolution equation}}
In order to make the attractor behaviour at early times manifest, more terms have to emerge in the equation that compete with the bulk stress at early times and introduce a dependence on the dynamical variable. The straightforward way to achieve this is by considering $f_\Pi=\Pi/\E$ as the dynamical variable. This is very similar to the dynamical variable used in the studies of the Bjorken flow attractor~\cite{Heller:2011ju,Heller:2015dha}. The chain rule will introduce another term $f_\Pi \E^{-1}\partial_t\E$ to the equation. As stated above, the coupling to the evolution of $\E$ does not spoil the arguments for the existence of dynamical fixed points. The evolution of $\E$ is, however, tied to that of $a$ and thereby introduces further terms that dominate at early times.

Now, in order for one of the new terms to compete with the bulk stress term, we want it to have the same time dependence. This is hindered by the additional $a$-dependence of $\zeta$. However, if we assume that $\tau_{\Pi}$ had the same dependence as $\zeta$, i.e. that $\chi/\E$ does not depend on $a$ and is (mostly) independent of $\E$, as would be the case for example if one were to compute it in kinetic theory of massive particles~\cite{DeGroot:1980dk} or in nonconformal holographic models~\cite{Buchel:2015ofa}, then the bulk stress term goes like $a(t)\partial_ta^{-1}(t)$. This is the same explicit time dependence as that of the term coming from the non-equilibrium contribution to the contact density in $\partial_t \E$. % This term will in fact have a quadratic dependence on $f_\Pi$ such that in total by the arguments above we should expect two early time fixed points, one attractive and one repulsive.
Neglecting the equilibrium part of the contact density, the evolution equation for $f_{\Pi}$ would be
%\frac{\zeta}{20\pi^{2/5}}\left(\frac{10}{\xi}\right)^{4/5}(me)^{-1/5}-\frac{\nu}{3\pi^{4/5}}\left(\frac{10}{\xi}\right)^{3/5}(me)^{-2/5}a^{-1}(t)
\begin{align}
    \partial_tf_\Pi&=\left[3f_\Pi^2-3(\chi/\E)\right]\ a\partial_ta^{-1} -\tau_\Pi^{-1}f_\Pi\;,\label{eq:fevo}
\end{align}
which would mean that $f_\Pi$ would have exactly the same attractor behaviour as the ratio of shear to energy density in the Bjorken flow case: at early times, there is one attractive and one repulsive fixed point at $f_\Pi=\pm\sqrt{\chi/\E}$ and at late times, there is only an attractive fixed point at $f_\Pi=0$. However, there are two key factors that modify this behaviour into something less trivial, as we will discuss in the following.

\vspace{10 pt}

\noindent \textbf{\emph{Differences with the Bjorken flow hydrodynamic attractor}} The assumptions we made in the previous section about  about the dependences of $\chi$ on other quantities in fact do not reflect reality. In particular, $\chi\propto a^{-2}$ has an additional dependence on time, which could make it dominant on a distinct timescale to the term that came from the rate of change of $\E$. However, if we set $a_0^{-1}=t_0^{-\alpha}$, then $a^{-1}(t)=\mathcal{O}(1)$ at early times and the two terms can still compete with each other. This would lead to the early time attractive value of $f_\Pi$ having a time dependence roughly as $a^{-1}(t)$ (but not exactly, since we can no longer neglect $\del_tf_\Pi $ as we did for fixed points).

It is possible to derive a more accurate statement about this early time attractor by rewriting the evolution equation into an equation in $a^{-1}$, making use of the fact that the chosen drive of $a^{-1}(t)$~\eqref{eq.formofexp} provides a bijection between $a^{-1}$ and $t$. Thus, we consider $f_\Pi(t)=\tilde{f}_\Pi(a^{-1}(t))$ and $\E(t)=\tilde{\E}(a^{-1}(t))$, which yields the evolution equations
\begin{align}
    \tilde{f}_\Pi'&=3\tilde{f}_\Pi^2a-3[(\chi a^2)/\tilde{\E}]a^{-1}-(\tau_\Pi\partial_ta^{-1})^{-1}\tilde{f}_\Pi\;,\\
    \tilde{\E}'&=-3\tilde{f}_\Pi\tilde{\E}a\;.
\end{align}
Now if at early times $a\partial_ta^{-1}\gg\tau_\Pi^{-1}$, then the relaxation term is negligible and the equation becomes entirely independent of the functional form of $a^{-1}(t)$. This means that the early time attractor behaviour depends only on the range in $a^{-1}$ that is covered, not on the specific form of the drive. Of course, if the system is observed as a function of $t$, then the drive still enters, but one would still find it in the same state at the same value of $a^{-1}$.

In this limit, assuming that $k\equiv3\sqrt{(\chi a^2)/\E}$ is a constant, the equation for $f_\Pi$ can actually be solved analytically to find
\begin{align}
    \tilde{f}_\Pi=\frac{k}{3}a^{-1}\frac{2K_1(ka^{-1})-cI_1(ka^{-1})}{2K_0(ka^{-1})+cI_0(ka^{-1})}
\end{align}
where $c=0$ corresponds to the attractor solution, distinguished by the fact that it is the only solution that is finite and positive as $a\to0$. Using properties of the modified Bessel function, we can approximate the functional form of the decay onto the attractor. For this, $ka^{-1}$ must be small enough for $cI_0(ka^{-1})\ll K_0(ka^{-1})$ but large enough for the approximation of $K_0$ to be valid.
\begin{align}
    &\tilde{f}_\Pi|_{c=0}-\tilde{f}_\Pi\\
    &=\frac{c}{3c_1I_0(ka^{-1})K_0(ka^{-1})+6K_0(ka^{-1})^2}\\&\approx\frac{c}{6K_0(ka^{-1})^2}\approx \frac{kc}{3\pi} a^{-1}\exp(2ka^{-1})
\end{align}

When eventually the relaxation term does become dominant, i.e. $\partial_tf_\Pi\approx-\tau_\Pi^{-1}f_\Pi$, it is easy to see that one would expect an exponential decay. This proceeds until the Navier-Stokes-limit is reached, where $f_\Pi$ is instantaneously given by the term $3[(\chi a^2)/\tilde{\E}]a^{-1}\partial_ta^{-1}$.


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig1a.pdf}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig1b.pdf}
    \caption{$f_\Pi(t)$-curves from solutions of the coupled evolution equations \eqref{eq:fevo} for $f_\Pi$ and \eqref{eq.dEdt} for $\E$ with initial conditions $f_\Pi(t_0)=0.1j$ and top: $\E(t_0)=\E_0$, bottom: $\E(t_0)=\E_0(1+j)$ for integer~$0\le j\le10$. Note that when looking at $f_\Pi$ exclusively, there is no attractor curve due to the coupling to $\E$.}
    \label{fig:earlyfatt}
\end{figure}

We show in Fig.~\ref{fig:earlyfatt} sets of solutions of the evolution equations starting at early times $t_0=10^{-2}$. Indeed, the top panel  seems to suggest that there is attractive behaviour towards a curve with the expected time dependence. However, the picture is less clear in the bottom panel. While this set of curves initially converges towards each other, they soon pass through each other and from there remain at some distance, keeping ratios roughly fixed. This is due to a further difference to the Bjorken flow case, specifically the fact that $\chi$ also depends on the energy density $\E$ in a nontrivial way. Thus, for different values of $\E$, the quantity $f_\Pi$ will evolve differently. In fact, since the evolution of $\E$ itself also depends on $f_\Pi$, curves that start with different initial $f_\Pi$ will develop differences in $\E$ and thereby also reach slightly different late time values of $f_\Pi$, explaining why even in the top panel the overlap of the different curves is not perfect.




One might expect that similar to the Bjorken flow case the curves can be made to overlap if one rescales time in a specific way. Indeed, if we formulate it in terms of $w=\tau T$, the evolution equation for the ratio of shear to energy density will completely decouple from the energy density in Bjorken flow. Specifically, one could rescale time in such a way that any unwanted factor of a power of energy density gets absorbed into the new drive $\tilde{a}^{-1}=\E^\kappa a^{-1}$. However, this idea is spoiled by the fact that $a^{-1}$ appears also in the evolution equation of $\E$. In particular, we get
\begin{align}
    \frac{\d\tilde{a}^{-1}}{\d a^{-1}}=\tilde{a}^{-1}-\frac{9}{5}\pi \E^{\kappa-1}\;,
\end{align}
so two terms containing different powers of $\E$. Thus, there is no hope of getting rid of the $\E$-dependence via rescaling of time. This procedure only works if energy density and the dynamical quantity in consideration evolve on the same timescale, which is trivially true in conformal scaling.

\vspace{10 pt}

\noindent \textbf{\emph{Similarities to Bjorken flow hydrodynamic attractor}} Despite all of the subtleties stated above, we can still identify attractive behaviour even for varying initial condition for $\E$. We just have to look at the problem through a different lens. Via $\chi$, the evolution of $f_\Pi$ is inevitably coupled to that of $\E$. We will therefore look at both quantities at the same time. In Fig.~\ref{fig:setatt}, we show a set of 10 000 solutions to the time evolution equations at three different times. We start with a square in the $(\E,\Pi)$-plane, but find that the time evolution deforms this subset of state space in such a way that it flattens in one direction and eventually becomes almost one-dimensional. Finally, we have found attractive behaviour that can similarly also be found in Bjorken flow.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig2a.png}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig2b.png}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig2c.png}
    \caption{Rectangular set of 10 000 solutions (gray) and three spherical sets of 5 000 solutions (blue, red, green) to the time evolution equations in the $(\E,\Pi)$-plane at times $t=t0$ (top), $t=0.05$ (middle) and $t=1$ (bottom) }
    \label{fig:setatt}
\end{figure}

In order to assess the speed of convergence, we follow~\cite{Heller:2020anv} and perform principal component analyses on sets of solutions. In order for the two directions to have similar magnitude and the same dimension, we consider the sets of solutions in $(\E,\Pi)$-space. We still solve the evolution equation for $f_\Pi$, as this turns out to be numerically advantageous.
We start with sets of 5 000 initial conditions in a sphere, having equal variance in the leading and subleading component. Fig.~\ref{fig:setatt} shows the spheres we considered in blue, red and green, though the results are very similar for all three sets, so we present them for only one of them.

Fig.~\ref{fig:pcr} shows the time evolution of the ratio of variances of the subleading to the leading component as a function of time for two different initialization times and three different values of $\alpha$. If now the full set of curves follows a collective behaviour, then distances between neighbouring curves will also exhibit this behaviour. Thus, in the variances of PCA, we can expect to see the square of the behaviour that we would have found for individual curves.

This is indeed what we observe, in the three stages advocated for in the introduction:
\begin{itemize}
\item \underline{STAGE E:} In the phase of convergence to the attractor the curves follow a behaviour $\sim [a^{-1}(t)\exp(2k a^{-1}(t))]^2$, which depends on $t$ only via $a$. The presence of this stage can be considered the key finding of our work.
\item \underline{STAGE T:} This is the phase of an exponential decay $\sim [\exp(-t/\tau_\Pi)]^2$, which is independent of the form of $a^{-1}(t)$.
\item \underline{STAGE H:} Finally, the curves reach the Navier-Stokes limit where they behave $\sim [a^{-1}(t)\partial_ta^{-1}(t)]^2$.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{10 pt}

\noindent \textbf{\emph{Conclusion and Outlook}} Hydrodynamic attractors are intrinsically nonequilibrium phenomena discovered in theoretical models of post-collision dynamics at RHIC and LHC accelerators. While hydrodynamic attractors do play a role in nuclear collisions phenomenology, e.g. for particle production estimates~\cite{Giacalone:2019ldn}, given the complexity of these processes no observables are known that would uni-vocally point to their emergence at high energies.

As a result, near-unitary Fermi gases, as proposed recently in~\cite{Fujii:2024yce}, offer currently the best prospects for experimental realization and better understanding of hydrodynamic attractors. Our main contribution to this novel area is detailing in a realistic model of a cold atomic gas how to realize the first, expansion-dominated stage of a hydrodynamic attractor, see STAGE~E in Fig.~\ref{fig:pcr}. 

Let us re-emphasize here that in order to observe the STAGE~E of the hydrodynamic attractor in the setup of cold atoms, one needs to track not only the bulk pressure~$\Pi$, but also the energy density~$\E$. This needs to be crucially taken into account in possible future experimental attempts.

Similarly to how phenomena such as nonthermal fixed points arise in the early time limit in nuclear collisions~\cite{Berges:2020fwq}, one should not expect STAGE~E to be there for arbitrarily fast driving. Indeed, it would be interesting both theoretically and experimentally to increase the rate of driving and see how STAGE~E gives way to other phenomena. On the theory side this requires going beyond the Drude form~\eqref{eq.bulkviscosity} and beyond the leading order terms in the large $k_{F} a$ and small $z$ expansions.

Finally, it would be interesting to generalize our analysis to incorporate periodic driving of~\cite{Mazeliauskas:2025jyi}, as it is the most natural to realize experimentally, and identify also in this setup the STAGE E.

