\section{An Independent, Identically Distributed AKLT Model}\label{sec:application}
In this section, we construct and analyze an explicit model of a Translation Co-variant State with small correlations. Certainly, one can recover from our theory the any deterministic translation-invariant MPS by taking all the transfer operators to be constant. Here, we reify a TCVS with `strong' on-site disorder. We accomplish this by beginning with the one-parameter family of states introduced in \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}, and pick a random value of the parameter at each site. In general, the dimension of the small correlation bundle need not be constant, and our model admits a Banach bundle description that is not locally trivial. We build on the existing theory to show that our IID-AKLT model admits a nearest-neighbor parent Hamiltonian that is measurable in the parameter and translation co-variant. Finally we use this to show the surprising fact that with probability one, the bulk gap of our IID-AKLT model vanishes. 


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preliminary Observations}\label{subsubsec:example_set_up}
An interesting family of examples comes from the one-parameter family of $C^*$-Finitely Correlated States studied in \cite[Examples 1-5, 7-8]{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}. Let us recall the setting, and then examine its properties from our point of view. 

Consider the spin-1 chain where $\mathcal{A} = \M_3$ consists of $3\times 3$ matrices at every site. Equip the local Hilbert spaces $\C^3$ with the basis of spin states $\ket{-1}, \ket{0}, \ket{1}$. Similarly, equip $\C^2$ with the spin-$\tfrac{1}{2}$ basis $\ket{-\tfrac{1}{2}}, \ket{+\tfrac{1}{2}}$. Following \cite[Example 1]{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}, for every $\omega \in [0, \pi)$, define the mapping $V_\omega: \C^2 \to \C^3 \otimes \C^2$ via
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{split}
            V_{\omega}\ket{\tfrac{1}{2}} &= \cos(\omega) \ket{1, - \tfrac12} - \sin(\omega) \ket{0,\tfrac12}\\
            V_\omega \ket{-\tfrac{1}{2}} &= \sin(\omega) \ket{0, -\tfrac12} - \cos(\omega) \ket{ -1, \tfrac 12}
        \end{split}
    \end{equation} 

We may realize $V_\omega$ as a $6\times 2$ matrix as (with respect to the convention that $\ket{+\frac{1}{2}} = [1\, 0]^T, \ket{-\frac{1}{2}} = [0\, 1]^T, \ket{+} = [1\, 0\,0]^T$, etc.)
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:V_matrix}
    V_\omega^* = \begin{bmatrix}
         0 & \cos(\omega) & -\sin(\omega) & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 0 & \sin(\omega) & -\cos(\omega) & 0
    \end{bmatrix}\,. 
\end{equation} We will sometimes write $s = \sin(\omega)$ and $c= \cos(\omega)$ for convenience. We may define the transfer operators 
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:AKLT_transfer}
    E_{a, \omega} = V^*_\omega ( a \otimes (\, \cdot\,))V_\omega
    \end{equation} which have continuously varying entries in $\omega$ and are completely positive whenever $a\in (\M_3)_+$. One checks \[
            E_{\one, z}(\one) = \one = E_{\one,z}^{\dagger}(\one)\,,
        \] where the dagger denotes the Hilbert-Schmidt adjoint of $E_{\one, z}$. Thus $E_{\one, \omega}$ is unit and trace preserving for all $\delta\in [0, \pi)$.

\begin{note}\label{note:pre_disorder_AKLT_state}
    Let $\vec z:=(z_m)_{m\in \Z}$ be a bi-infinite sequence of angles in $[0, \pi)$, where we identify $\pi$ and $0$. Define the state $\nu_{\vec{z}}$ on $(\mathcal{A})_{\Z}$ by the equations: 
        \begin{equation}\label{eqn:delta_state}
            \nu_{\vec{z}}(a_n \otimes \cdots \otimes a_m) = \frac{1}{2}\tr\left[E_{a_n, \delta_n}\circ \cdots \circ E_{a_m, \delta_m}(\one)\right] 
        \end{equation} where the $E_{a,\delta}$ are defined in equation~(\ref{eqn:AKLT_transfer}) for $a\in \M_2$ and $\delta \in [0, \pi)$. 
\end{note}

Since $E_{\one, z}$ is unital and trace-preserving, the formula~(\ref{eqn:delta_state}) is consistent with $*$-homomorphisms associated to volumetric inclusions. By Takeda's theorem~(Theorem~\ref{thm:Takeda}), we have a well-defined bulk state on all $(\mathcal{A})_\Z$, whose coefficients in the computational basis are given by a matrix product state expansion with site-dependent angles. The map $E_{\one, z}$ plays a distinguished role in the following and so we shall write $\phi_z$ to emphasize this. 

Recall that with respect to the Hilbert-Schmit orntho-normal basis $\{\one, \sigma^z, \sigma^+, \sigma^-\} \subset \M_2$ formed from the Pauli Matrices, that the matrix $M$ defining $\phi_z$ is diagonal with
    \begin{equation}
        M = \begin{bmatrix}
            1 & & & \\
             & \sin^2(z) - \cos^2(z) & &\\
             & & -\sin^2(z) &\\
             &&& -\sin^2(z)
        \end{bmatrix}\,.
    \end{equation}
\begin{lem}\label{lem:translation_operator_strictly_positive}
    Let $\phi_z = E_{\one, z}\in B(\M_2)$ be the transfer operator defined above. Fix constants $0<c_1<c_2 < \frac{\pi}{2}$ and let $\vec{z}\in ((0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi))^\Z$. Put $\Phi_{m,n} = \phi_{z_m} \circ \cdots \circ \phi_{z_n}$ and let $T:\M_2 \to \M_2$ be the replacement channel $T(b) = \tr_2(b) \one$, where $\tr_2$ is the normalized trace. For every $\mu \in (0,1)$ and $x\in [m,n]$, there is a  a constant $c_{\mu, x}>0$ so that  
        \begin{equation}
            \|\Phi_{m,n} - T\| = \max\left\{\prod_{j=m}^n |\cos(2z_j)|, \prod_{j=m}^n \sin^2(z_j)\right\} \le c_{\mu,x}\, \cdot \mu^{n-m}
        \end{equation}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
The first equality is essentially immediate from the fact that $\Phi_{n,m}$ is diagonalizable. To obtain the upper estimate, one first checks that $\Phi_{m,n}$ is \emph{eventually strictly positive} in the sense of \cite[Assumption 1]{MovassaghSchenker}. Fix an $\omega \in (0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup (\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi)$. We aim to show that $\phi_\omega$ is a strictly positive map in the sense that $A\in (\M_2)_+$ implies $\phi_\omega(A)>0$.  

Recall \cite{Bhatia_MA, Bhatia_PDM}, a nonzero $2\times 2$ matrix is positive semidefinite if and only if $\tr A = a+c >0$ and $\det A \ge 0$, and moreover if $A$ is positive definite if and only if $\det A >0$. Let $A = \begin{bmatrix}
    a& b\\b^* &c
\end{bmatrix}$ be a positive semidefinite matrix which is nonzero.  Notice, since $\tr$ is faithful,  $a+c >0$, whence at least one of them is nonzero. Similarly, $\det A = ac-|b|^2  \ge 0$.

One can compute explicitly 
\[
    \det \phi_\omega(A) = (a^2 + c^2)\sin^2 \omega\,  \cos^2\omega + a \cos^4\omega + (ac-|b|^2) \sin^4 \omega >0
\] for any value of $\omega$ in range provided one of $a,c$ is nonzero. Since $\phi_\omega$ is trace preserving, we know that $\phi_\omega$ is a strictly positive map for any $\omega$ in range. Moreover, since $\phi_\omega$ is trace-preserving, it is faithful, thus $\ker \phi_\omega \cap (\M_2)_+ = \{0\}$, and similarly since $\phi_\omega$ is unital the Schr\"odinger picture adjoint satisfies $\ker \phi_\omega^\dagger \cap (M_2)_+ = \{0\}$.

Now, the upper estimate follows from the analysis leading up to \cite[Theorem 2]{MovassaghSchenker}.
\end{proof}

\begin{note}\label{note:Matrices_for_Gamma}
    We note that with respect to the canonical bases, one may define the matrices $X^-(z), X^0(z), X^+(z)$ as follows
        \[
            [X^{(j)}(z)]_{k,\ell = 1}^2 := \<j, k| V_z|\ell\>; \quad j\in \{+, 0, -\}
        \] One obtains the expressions:
        \begin{equation}\label{eqn:AKLT_Kraus}
    X^{+}(z) := \begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 0\\ \cos(z) & 0\\
    \end{bmatrix}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,  X^{0}(z):= \begin{bmatrix}
        -\sin(z) & 0 \\
        0 & \sin(z)\\
    \end{bmatrix}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,   X^{-}(z) := \begin{bmatrix}
        0 & -\cos(z)\\
        0 & 0\\
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{equation} We remark that \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner} define matrices $v^{(j)}$ similarly, and the relation between our notations is that $X^{(j)}(z) = [v^{(j)}(z)]^*$. When convenient, we will often write $X^{(j)}_z$ instead of using function notation. 
\end{note}

\begin{note}\label{note:Gamma_map}
Consider an interval $[m,n] \subset \Z$. We define a family of mappings from $\M_2$ into the local Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H}^{[m,n]} \cong (\C^3)^{\otimes (n-m)}$ via 
 \begin{equation}\label{eqn:gamma_mn}
    \Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(b) = \sum_{i_m, \dots, i_n \in \{-, 0, +\}}\, \tr[b X^{i_n}_{z_n} X^{i_{n-1}}_{z_{n-1}}\cdot \cdots \cdot X^{i_{m}}_{z_m}]\, \ket{i_m, \dots, i_n}\,
 \end{equation} where $\ket{i_m, \dots, i_n}$ is the notation for the computational basis in $\mathcal{H}^{[m,n]}$. 
\end{note}
We remark that the convention of some authors is to find matrices $Y^{(j)}$ that represent the state where the multiplication inside the trace is taken in the order $Y^{i_m} Y^{i_{m-1}} \cdots Y^{i_n}$ (see e.g. \cite{Perez-Garcia_et_al, Tasaki_book}). Since we appeal to formulas in \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}, we shall conform to their conventions for the convenience of the reader. 


Notice that for all $\vec{z}\in [0, \pi)^{\Z}$ the composition of transfer operators given by 
 \begin{equation}\label{eqn:z_iterate_transfer}
     E_{a_n \otimes \cdots \otimes a_m, \vec{z}}^{[n,m]}:= E_{a_n, z_n}\circ \cdots \circ E_{a_m, z_m}:\M_2 \to \M_2
 \end{equation} for $a_n, \dots, a_m \in \M_3$ extends to a linear operator on $\mathcal{A}_{[n,m]}$ linearly (see Lemma~\ref{lem:iterate_hom}, the proof is essentially the same). 


We have the following lemmas. 
\begin{lem}\label{lem:gamma_formulas}
    Let $z \mapsto (V_z : \C^k \otimes \C^k \to \C^k)$ be the continuous isometry-valued function defined in Equation~(\ref{eqn:V_matrix}). Let $m<n$ be integers and $\vec{z}\in [0, \pi)^{\Z}$.  Let $\{\ket{j}\}_{j\in \{-,0,+\}}$ denote the canoncal basis of $\C^3$ and $\{\ket{k}\}_{k\in \{-\frac12, +\frac12\}}$ denote the canonical basis of $\C^2$. With notation as in~\ref{note:Matrices_for_Gamma} and~\ref{note:Gamma_map}, and equation~(\ref{eqn:z_iterate_transfer}) above, the following hold. 
    \begin{enumerate}[label = \alph*.)]
        \item For all $m<n\in \Z$ and every $a\in \mathcal{A}_{[m,n]} \cong (\M_3)^{\otimes (n-m)}$ the transfer operator $E^{[m,n]}_{a,\vec{z}}: \M_2 \to \M_2$ may be expressed as 
        \begin{equation}
            E^{[m,n]}_{a,\omega}(b) = \sum_{\substack{i_m, \cdots, i_n\\ j_m, \dots, j_n}} \<i_m, \dots, i_n|\, a\, |j_m, \dots, j_n\> (X^{i_m}_{z_m})^* \cdots (X^{i_n}_{z_n})^*\,\cdot  b\,\cdot X^{j_n}_{z_n} \cdots X^{j_m}_{z_m}\,.
        \end{equation} 
        \item One has that for all $a\in \mathcal{A}_{[m, n]}$ and all $x, y\in \M_2$, the following expectation value on $\mathcal{H}^{[m,n]}$ has the expansion
        \begin{equation}
            \<\Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(x)|\, a\,|\Gamma_{\vec{z}}^{[m,n]}(y)\>_{\mathcal{H}^{[m,n]}} = \sum_{j_1,\,j_2\in \{\pm \frac 12\}} \<j_1|\, E^{[m, n]}_{a,\vec{z}}\left(\,x^* \ket{j_1}\bra{j_2}y\, \right)\, | j_2\>_{\C^2}
        \end{equation} In particular, putting $\rho = \tfrac{1}{2}\one_{2\times 2}$, and letting $\langle x, y \rangle_\rho = \tr[\rho x^*y]$, we have the estimate 
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:rho_product_estimate}
           |\<\Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(x), \Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(y)\>_{\mathcal{H}} - \<x,y\>_\rho|\le 2\|\Phi_{m,n}-T\|\,.
        \end{equation}
        \item For all $\vec{z}\in ((0,\frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2},\pi))^{\Z}$ and all $m\le n$, and all $x,y\in \M_2$, we have 
        \begin{equation}
            |\<\Gamma^{[m,n]}_z(x)| a | \Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(y)\> - \tfrac{1}{2}\nu(a) \<x|y\>_{\HS}| \le   \|\Phi_{m,n} - T\|\, \|a\| \|x\|_{\HS}\|y\|_{\HS}   \end{equation}
            More generally, if $\supp(a) = [m',n']\subset [m,n]$, then, for all $x,y\in \M_2$ with $\|x\|_{\HS}, \|y\|_{\HS} \le 1$, we have
        \begin{equation}\label{eqn:approximate_nu}
            |\<\Gamma^{[m,n]}_z(x)| \one_{[m, m'-1]}\otimes a\otimes \one_{[n'+1, n]} | \Gamma^{[m,n]}_{\vec{z}}(y)\> - \tfrac{1}{2}\nu(a) \<x|y\>_{\HS}| \le  ( \|\Phi_{m,m'-1} - T\| + \|\Phi_{n'+1, n} - T\|) \, \|a\| 
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lem} 

The proofs are essentially the same as those in Lemma 5.1 of \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner} so we will not repeat them. We do note, however, that a factor of $\tfrac 12$ in~\ref{eqn:approximate_nu} comes from the fact that $E_{\one, z}$ is trace preserving, whence the density matrix it preserves is $\tfrac 12$. 

We now aim to show that the $\Gamma^{[m,n]}$-maps are injective for every $\vec{z}\in ((0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi))^\Z$ whenever $n-m\ge 2$. In the language of \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}, we will show that $\nu_{\vec{z}}$ has an interaction length of $2$ for all $\vec{z}$ which avoid $0$ and $\frac{\pi}{2}$.


Recall \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner, Perez-Garcia_et_al} that the subspace \begin{equation}\label{eqn:ground-state-space}
        \Gamma^{[j, j+1]}_{\vec{z}}(\M_2) := \mathcal{G}_{j,j+1} = \left\{ \sum_{i_j, i_{j+1}} \tr(bX_{z_{j+1}}^{i_{j+1}}X^{i_{j}}_{z_{j}}) |i_j, i_{j+1}\rangle \colon b\in \M_2 \right\} \subset (\C^3)_{j} \otimes (\C^3)_{j+1}\,.
    \end{equation} The subspace $\mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}$ determines the support of a positive operator $P_{j, j+1}$ so that the two-point restriction of $\nu_{\vec{z}}$ satisfies the condition $\nu_{\vec{z}}(P_{j, j+1})=0$. We aim to show that for any $\vec{z} \in ((0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi))^\Z$ that $\Gamma^{[j, j+1]}_{\vec{z}}$ is injective. That is, the rank of $\mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}$ is equal to four. 

    Rearranging equation~(\ref{eqn:ground-state-space})  a bit, we see that \[
        \mathcal{G}_{j,j+1} = \underset{0\le k, \ell \le 2}{\spn}\left\{ \sum_{i_j, i_{j+1}} \<k|X^{i_{j+1}}_{z_{j+1}}X^{i_j}_{z_j}|\ell\>\, \cdot |i_j, i_{j+1}\rangle\right\}
    \]

    Therefore, a basis for $\mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}$ can be constructed from the corresponding entries of the $3\times 3$ array of matrix products $(X^{i_{j+1}}_{z_{j+1}}X^{i_j}_{z_j})_{i_j,i_{j+1}\in \{-, 0, +\}}$. This results in the following basis vectors which vary as functions in $\vec{z}$ and $j$:
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:gj_basis}
        \begin{split}
            |\phi_{11}(j)\> &= s_js_{j+1} |00\> - c_jc_{j+1}|+-\>\\
            |\phi_{12}(j)\> &= c_j s_{j+1} \ket{-0} - s_j c_{j+1} \ket{0-}\\
            |\phi_{21}(j)\> &= c_j s_{j+1}|+0\> - s_j c_{j+1} |0+\>\\
            |\phi_{22}(j)\> &= s_j s_{j+1} |00\> - c_j c_{j+1} |-+\>\\
        \end{split}
    \end{equation} where we have written $c_j = \cos( z_j)$ and $s_j = \sin(z_j)$ for convenience. We have therefore shown the following. 
    \begin{lem}
        Let $\vec{z}$ be any bi-infinite sequence taking values in in $(0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi)$ and let $\mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}$ be the ground state space associated to $\Gamma^{[j, j+1]}_{\vec{z}}$. Then the rank of $\mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}$ is identically four for $\vec{z}$ in range. 
    \end{lem}

As noted in \cite{FannesNachtergaeleWerner}, injectivity of the $\Gamma^{[n,m]}$ maps at a short distance does not guarantee they remain injective as $|n-m|$ increases. In order to check this, we must verify that the frustration free intersection property is verified. Recall that the three-site ground-state space is given by
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{G}_{j, j+1, j+2} := \spn\left\{ \sum_{i_j, i_{j+1}, i_{j+2}} \tr\left[b X^{i_{j+2}}_{z_{j+2}}\, X^{i_{j+1}}_{z_{j+1}}\, X^{i_j}_{z_j}\right] |i_j, i_{j+1}, i_{j+2} \rangle \right\}
    \end{equation}

\begin{lem}\label{lem:delta_MPS_injectivity}
    Let $\vec{z} = (z_{m})_{m\in \Z}$ be a bi-infinite sequence taking values in $(0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup(\frac{\pi}{2}, \pi)$ and let $\nu_{\vec{z}}$ be the corresponding state constructed as in~(\ref{eqn:delta_state}). Then, for any site $x\in \Z$,
        \[
            \mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}\otimes (\C^3)_{j+2}\cap (\C^3)_x\otimes \mathcal{G}_{j+1, j+2} = \mathcal{G}_{j, j+1, j+2}.
        \] Thus, the interaction length of $\nu_{\vec{z}}$ is $\ell =2$. Furthermore, for any $\vec{z}$ in range and $n-m>2$, one has 
        \begin{equation}\label{eqn:intersect_ground_state}
            \mathcal{G}_{[m,n]} = \bigcap_{j=m}^{n-2} \mathcal{H}_{[m,j]}\otimes \mathcal{G}_{j, j+1}\otimes \mathcal{H}_{[j+2, n]}
        \end{equation} where we adopt the convention that when $j=m$, the symbol $\mathcal{H}_{[m,m]}$ is interpereted to mean $(\C^3)^{\otimes 0}$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
    After possibly re-indexing, it is sufficient to show that the intersection property holds for three consecutive sites $x, x+1$ and $x+2$ which we will abbreviate as $1,2$ and $3$ for convenience. We therefore adopt the notational convention that $c_1 = \cos(\delta_x)$, $c_2 = \cos(\delta_{x+1})$, $c_3 = \cos(\delta_{x+2})$, and similarly for the sine values. 

    Observe that $\mathcal{G}_{1,2,3} \subset \mathcal{G}_{1,2}\otimes (\C^3)_{3}\cap (\C^3)_1\otimes \mathcal{G}_{2,3}$. Furthermore, the explicit computation of the basis for $\mathcal{G}_{1,2}$ in~(\ref{eqn:gj_basis}), implies $\dim(\mathcal{G}_{1,2,3})\ge \max\{\dim(\mathcal{G}_{1,2}), \dim(\mathcal{G}_{2,3})\}$. Thus, to show equality of the spaces we need to show $$\dim(\mathcal{G}_{1,2}\otimes \C^3 \cap \C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2,3}) \le \max\{\dim(\mathcal{G}_{1,2}),\dim(\mathcal{G}_{2,3})\}\,.$$ 

    To do so, we aim to find a convenient decomposition of each space that will allow us to directly compute the dimension of the overlap. 

    First, notice $S^z_{3}$ commutes with the total spin operator $S^2_{1, 2}$, and similarly with $S^z_1$ and $S_{2, 3}^2$. Thus, we may decompose $\mathcal{G}_{1,2} = \bigoplus_{k=-2}^2 \mathcal{G}_{1, 2}^{(k)}$ where each subspace $\mathcal{G}_{1, 2}^{(k)}$ is contained in the eigensubspace of $S^2_{1, 2}$ corresponding to $k$. However, we note that from~(\ref{eqn:gj_basis}) that $\mathcal{G}_{1, 2}^{(\pm 2)} = \{0\}$. Thus, 
    \[
        \mathcal{G}_{1, 2} \otimes \C^3 = \bigoplus_{k=-1}^1 \bigoplus_{m=-1}^1 \mathcal{G}_{1,2}^{(k)} \otimes \C|m\> = \bigoplus_{k+m = -2}^2 \mathcal{G}_{1,2}^{(k)} \otimes \C|m\>\,.
    \] We may perform a similar decomposition on $\C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}$. 

    We have therefore written $\mathcal{G}_{1, 2} \otimes \C^3$ and $\C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}$ in the eigenbasis of $S_1^z \otimes S_{2}^z \otimes S_{3}^z$. But this operator commutes with the total spin $S^2_{1, 2, 3}$. Therefore, each subspace $\mathcal{G}_{1, 2} \otimes \C|m\>$ is contained in the eigenspace corresponding to the quantum number $k+m$, and similarly for $\C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}$. 

    All this to say that the intersection admits a direct sum decomposition 
    \[
        \mathcal{G}_{1,2}\otimes \C^3 \cap \C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3} = \bigoplus_{k+m=-2}^2 \mathcal{G}^{(k)}_{1, 2} \otimes |m\> \cap |m\> \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}^{(k)}. 
    \]

    Now, to estimate the dimension of $\mathcal{G}_{1,2}\otimes \C^3 \cap \C^3 \otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}$ it is sufficient to estimate the dimension of each direct summand corresponding to one of the spin values $k = \pm 2, \pm 1, 0$. To do so, we will explicitly compute a basis in both the left-hand sum $L(k):= \bigoplus_{m\colon k+m\in \{\pm 1, 0\}}\mathcal{G}_{1, 2}^{(k)} \otimes |m\>$ and the right-hand summand $R(k):= \bigoplus_{m\colon k+m\in \{\pm 1, 0\}}|m\>\otimes \mathcal{G}_{2, 3}^{(k)}$. We use these basis expansions to determine the largest possible dimension of $L(k)\cap R(k)$ (namely, by symbolically computing the null space of an appropriate matrix). To make the resulting tables more legible, we adopt the convention that $\ell_{x,y} := \phi_{x,y}(j)$ and $r_{x,y} := \phi_{x,y}(j+1)$, where $\phi_{x,y}$ is defined in equation~(\ref{eqn:gj_basis}) above. 

    In the case of spin $+2$, the resulting subspaces  $\mathcal{G}_{j,j+1}^{(1)}\otimes \C\ket{+}$ and $\C\ket{+}\otimes \mathcal{G}_{j+1,j+2}^{(1)}$ are one-dimensional spanned by $\ket{\ell_{21},+}= \ket{\phi_{21}(j),+}$ and $\ket{+,r_{21}}=\ket{+, \phi_{21}(j+1)}$. Expanding both vectors, one obtains
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{split}
            \ket{\phi_{21}(j), +} & = c_1 s_2 \ket{+0+} - s_1 c_2 \ket{0++}\\
            \ket{+, \phi_{21}(j+1)}&= c_2 s_3 \ket{++0}- s_2 c_3 \ket{+0+}\,
        \end{split}
    \end{equation} Since $z_1, z_2, z_3 \in (0, \frac{\pi}{2})\cup (0, \pi)$, it must be that  $c_1s_2 \neq 0$, and $c_2s_3\neq 0$, whence these vectors are are linearly independent and therefore have no common vector in their intersection. 

    The case of spin $-2$ is similar: the left-hand side is spanned by $\ket{\phi_{12}(j), -} = c_1s_2\ket{-0-} - s_1 c_2 \ket{0--}$ and the right hand side is spanned by $\ket{-, \phi_{12}(j+1)} = c_2s_3\ket{--0}-s_2c_3\ket{-0-}$, which are linearly independent. 

    Now, in the case that $k+m= +1$, the allowed values of are as follows: $(k,m) = (1,0), (0,1)$. We re-organize this information by $m$-value in the following table. The resulting basis elements of $L(+1)$ with total spin 1 can be written as 

    \begin{center}
        \begin{tabular}{c|c}
            $m$& basis vector of $L(+1)$ with spin 1\\
            \hline \hline
            $-$&  $\emptyset$\\
            $0$&  $v_1:= |\ell_{21}, 0\> = -s_j c_{j+1} |0+0\> + c_j s_{j+1} |+00\>$\\
            $+$ &  \begin{tabular}{@{}c@{}}
                    $v_2: = |\ell_{11}, +1\> = s_j s_{j+1} |00+\> - c_jc_{j+1}|+-+\>$\\
                    $v_3 := |\ell_{22}, +\> = s_j s_{j+1} |00+\> - c_j c_{j+1} |-++\>$
            \end{tabular}\\
        \end{tabular}
    \end{center}

    Similarly for $R(+1)$, we get a family of basis vectors: 
    \begin{center}
            \begin{tabular}{c|c}
            $m$& basis vector of $R(+1)$ with spin 1 \\
            \hline \hline
            $-$&  $\emptyset$\\
            $0$&  $w_1:= |0, r_{21}\> = -s_{j+1} c_{j+2} |00+\> + c_{j+1} s_{j+2} |0+0\>$\\
            $+$ &  \begin{tabular}{@{}c@{}}
                    $w_2: = |+1,r_{11}\> = s_{j+1} s_{j+2} |+00\> - c_{j+1}c_{j+2}|++-\>$\\
                    $w_3 := |+1,r_{22}\> = s_{j+1} s_{j+2} |+00\> - c_{j+1} c_{j+2} |+-+\>$
            \end{tabular}\\
        \end{tabular}
    \end{center}
    Now, if there is a vector in $L(+1) \cap R(+1)$, then it is a linear combination of $\{v_1, v_2, v_3\}$ and $\{w_1, w_2, w_3\}$. By passing to the spin-z basis we observe that such a vector is an element of the null space of the $6\times 6$ matrix in Table~\ref{tab:s_+1-matrix} (where we have left blank the cells which contain a zero for legibility).
    \begin{table}[h]
        \centering
        \begin{tabular}{c||c|c|c|c|c|c}
             & $v_1$ & $v_2$ & $v_3$ & $-w_1$ & $-w_2$ & $-w_3$\\
             \hline \hline
             $\ket{-++}$& & & $-c_jc_{j+1}$ & & \\
             $\ket{0+0}$& $-s_jc_{j+1}$ & & & $-c_{j+1}s_{j+2}$ &\\
             $\ket{00+}$& & $s_js_{j+1}$ & $s_js_{j+1}$ & $s_{j+1}c_{j+2}$ & \\
             $\ket{+00}$& $c_js_{j+1}$ & & & & $-s_{j+1}s_{j+2}$ & $-s_{j+1}s_{j+2}$ \\
             $\ket{+-+}$& & $-c_jc_{j+1}$ & & & &$c_{j+1}c_{j+2}$\\
             $\ket{++-}$& & & & & $c_{j+1}c_{j+2}$ &\\
        \end{tabular}
        \caption{Spin +1 overlap matrix}
        \label{tab:s_+1-matrix}
    \end{table}  Entering this matrix into Mathematica and using the \url{NullSpace} command returns a null space spanned by the vector: 
        \begin{equation}
            \nu_1:= 2s_{3} v_1 + c_{3}v_2 = 2s_1 w_1 - c_1 w_3\,
        \end{equation} Fully expanding we see that 
        \[
            \nu_1 = -2s_1c_2s_3\ket{0+0} + 2c_1s_2s_3 \ket{+00} +s_1s_2c_3\ket{00+} - c_1c_2c_3\ket{+-+}\,,
        \] from which we see that the only way for the dimension to collapse to zero is if each coefficient equals zero, which is not possible for our choices of $z_1,z_2z_3$. Thus, we conclude $\dim(L(+1) \cap R(+1)) =1$. 

        Repeating this process in the case of total spin $-1$, the resulting $6\times 6$ matrix given in Table~\ref{tab:s_-1-matrix}. Performing the same computation as above, we see that the null space has dimension at most one and is generated by the vector 
        \begin{equation}
