In particular, it follows that there are infinitely many regular maps of Euler characteristic $-r^d$ when $r$ is an odd prime if $d\in \{1,3\}$, but there are only a finite number of such maps if $d\in \{2,4\}$.

The rest of this paper is structured as follows. In Section \ref{s: chi odd} we present general structural theory for the automorphism group $G$ of a regular map on a (non-orientable) surface with odd Euler characteristic $\chi_G$. Then in Sections \ref{s: insol-PSL} and \ref{s: insol-PGL} we use this theory to consider the cases of such groups $G$ when $\chi_G=-r^d$ and $G/O(G)$ is isomorphic to $\PSL_2(q)$ or $\PGL_2(q)$ for some prime-power $q\ge 5$, followed by an analysis of soluble groups $G$ with $\chi_G=-r^d$ in Section \ref{s: soluble}. These three sections yield a proof of Theorem~\ref{t: main}. Finally, in Section \ref{s: constructions} we present constructions for automorphism groups $G$ of regular maps with $\chi_G=-r^d$, and give a proof of Corollary~\ref{c: main}.
