and finally to operate a conditional dilation \cite{Serra88} of these centerlines inside connected lumens to reconstruct each lumen. 
In fact, each step leads to difficulties. The main problem is that the computing of the shortest path between two points of the same lumen may produce a path that is not only included inside a single lumen but also takes the second lumen through the tears.

This is particularly the case in the object of Fig. \ref{fig:fig4} (obtained from a real image) for which the two lumens are intertwined around two intimal tears : two anchor points (start/arrival) are defined in the first lumen (points 1 and 6); the found path when running the shortest path algorithm starts from point 1 and reaches point 2 in the same first lumen, then crosses at a first tear to reach the second lumen at point 3, then goes to point 4 still in the second lumen, then again crosses a second tear again to reach the first lumen at point 5 and then to reach point 6 of arrival. Here we have the example of a shortest path that changes twice the lumen. 

