The pre-encounter velocities of 1I and 2I each lie within a moving group, as is predicted for most ISOs in the \OOshort.
However, 3I's large vertical velocity \(W\) places it outside of the main moving group structures, which are made up of only the low-vertical-motion stars that are more affected by Galactic structure resonances \citep{Daniel2015} .
%large W is the cause of its unexpected southern hemisphere radiant)
This large value of \(W\) is the cause of its extreme southern radiant, and places 3I's origin in the Milky Way's thick disk, a dynamically and chemically distinct structure from the thin disk to which most stars, including the Sun, belong \citep{Bovy2016}.
It seems likely that 3I is our first ISO from this component of the Milky Way. 

